\section{Research attempt}

\subsection{Literature, source context and scope}
The full catalogue body, including qualifications and historical source status, is preserved above. The original workshop report was inspected in the locally cached publisher PDF text. This attempt distinguishes the source question from any explicitly restricted model introduced below. Repeated records retain their own source IDs and do not become independent conjectures. Elementary results and attributed literature results are not claimed as new original-target progress.

\subsection{A separate attempted route for the repeated record}
This fragment repeats the dimension-parameterized containment question. The external W[1]-hardness result below materially qualifies its dated literature assessment. We test the common proposal to enumerate all sign regions of generator scalar products and describe that as FPT simply because dimension is fixed.
For $n\ge d\ge1$, choose normals $g_i=(1,t_i,\ldots,t_i^{d-1})$ with $t_i=i$. Any $d$ are independent: their determinant is the nonzero Vandermonde product. Let $R(n,d)$ be the number of full-dimensional regions cut out by the central hyperplanes $g_i^Tu=0$.
\begin{proposition}
\[
 R(n,d)=2\sum_{j=0}^{d-1}\binom{n-1}{j}.
\]
Thus explicit region enumeration on this family is not bounded by $f(d)n^C$ for any fixed exponent $C$ and finite function $f$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
In dimension one there are two regions. If $n\le d$ independent hyperplanes are linearly equivalent to coordinate hyperplanes and produce $2^n$ regions, agreeing with the formula using zero binomial coefficients. For $n>d\ge2$, add the last hyperplane. The old hyperplanes restrict to a central arrangement on it in dimension $d-1$, with every $d-1$ restricted normals independent because any $d$ original normals are independent. Each region of the restricted arrangement cuts one old region into two, so
$R(n,d)=R(n-1,d)+R(n-1,d-1)$.
Induction and Pascal's identity yield the formula. For fixed $d$, it grows as a positive constant times $n^{d-1}$. Given $C$, fix $d>C+1$ and let $n$ increase; the ratio $R(n,d)/n^C$ is unbounded, contradicting any such enumeration bound.
\end{proof}
Within each region every sign is fixed, so the segment support expression is linear there. This explains the attractiveness of enumeration, but also its dimension-dependent exponent. The argument counts explicit regions; it is not a lower bound against algorithms that implicitly handle many regions at once.

\subsection{Verification and remaining gaps}
The recurrence and formula were checked for $1\le d\le5$ and $1\le n\le10$; for $n=10,d=3$ the count is 92. The repeated source question is not solved by labeling an XP-style enumeration as FPT, and the external conditional hardness is not reproved here.

